{
  "dom_id": "step-q75724",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (but not $1$).\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4$ (but not $5$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(-3v)=-3(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2v)=-3(2v),$ and $2v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
